often i find out about weird exam  questions from comments left on my  videos and today's story starts back in  january when i noticed a handful of  comments on my short film called finding  x  saying that the video reminded them of  an essay prompt from the university of  chicago i was quite perplexed by that  and i was intrigued to know how my  fictional story about numbers could  possibly remind someone of an academic  essay prompt but what i found is that  students applying for the university of  chicago have to write a few essays and  one of them must respond to a set of  pretty weird prompts let me show you  what i mean  last year's prompts include what if the  moon were made of cheese or neptune made  of soap pick a celestial object  re-imagine its material composition and  explore the implications  they also asked what's so easy about pie  and to invent a new unit of measurement  they suggest that answers to these  questions should be about 650 words  although there isn't actually a hard  limit and you can see for each question  they credit a current university of  chicago student because they are the  ones who submit the ideas for the  questions this one quotes mark twain and  says there is no such thing as a new  idea and asks are any pieces of art  literature philosophy or technology  truly original or just a different  combination of old ideas pick something  anything besides yourself and explain  why it is or is not original going back  through the previous year's questions  they have this classic one here  find x which is the one that people had  been reminded of by my video i suppose  that writing a poem about x going off on  a journey to find their value would be  one way to answer the question but from  what i can tell the purpose of asking  these types of questions is to give  students an opportunity to share  something about themselves and their  ambitions the admissions team would have  to read thousands of these so you do  want your essay to stand out it's  tempting to read through the questions  and try to think of the most creative  and wacky ways to answer them to show  that you're thinking outside the box  and that's probably great no one wants  to sound too generic but it's probably  more important to focus on trying to  show genuine passion for your course of  study at the bare minimum you'd want to  let the admissions team know that you  aren't just applying because your  parents are forcing you to and that  secretly you'd rather be going somewhere  else or studying something different if  i were to turn my poem into an essay to  answer the prompt find x i would  probably lean in more to one of my  favorite lines which was math is not  about your value math is about  relationships  i might try to show that that line also  applies to life itself and talk about  the creative nature of math that it's  not just about finding the value of x  but about coming up with creative  relationships between numbers to  describe the world around us find x  shares the stage with so where is waldo  really  and everyone knows there are two types  of people in the world what are they  there is how did you get caught or not  caught as the case may be  and in 650 words describe a picture and  explore what it wants  no question here just a quote  don't play what's there play what's not  there  some of these questions are a little bit  absurd but this one shows some  self-awareness it says that the only  interesting answers are those that  destroy the questions  we have all heard serious questions  absurd questions and seriously absurd  questions some of which cannot be  answered without obliterating the very  question  destroy a question with your answer  for the aspiring mathematical  philosophers we have what can actually  be divided by zero  then we have the inspirational question  what's so odd about odd numbers  there is some more serious than others  but they all leave room for a lot of  creativity  beneath this one asking us to use the  power of string to explain the biggest  or smallest phenomenon  there is this one here asking us to  write an essay somehow inspired by super  huge mustard and then one which is  closer to home for me in 2015 the city  of melbourne australia created a tree  mail service in which all of the trees  in the city received an email address so  that residents could report any tree  related issues as an unexpected result  people began to email their favorite  trees sweet and occasionally humorous  letters  imagine this has been expanded to any  object tree or otherwise in the world  and share with us the letter you'd send  to your favorite my friend julian o'shea  might ace this one he's made a whole  video about emailing trees  apparently current students submit the  suggestion as a hot dog a sandwich so  often that they finally had to write a  question inspired by it a hot dog might  be a sandwich and cereal might be a soup  but is a blank a blank and i really like  this question under it here  fiction reveals truth that reality  obscures i really like it because i came  across a similar quote recently and it  helped me to put into words why i think  fiction is sometimes worth reading and  why you can learn things from books even  if they are fictional i did find some  essays written by students who are  successful in their application i  particularly like this answer posted to  collegeadvisor.com  answering a prompt about the mantis  shrimp and its amazingly complex eyes  with 16 different types of color  receptors  they ask what might the mantis shrimp be  able to see that we cannot  what are we missing  i really like this answer it is a bit  more serious but it offers some new  perspectives to both the good and bad  things that sight has given us  they talk about the discrimination that  can be attributed to our sense of vision  and of the ways that sight could be  considered limiting and they bring it  all back to ideas from the book flatland  which explores the challenge of  explaining higher realms to those who  cannot experience it the king of  pointland is so narrow-minded and  insular that he refuses to believe that  there are objects larger than his when  confronted with a square all he sees is  another point as humans our abilities  are limited as well we do not have the  innate skills of the mantis shrimp with  its 16 receptors however centuries of  innovation have made us inept to fully  perceive the skills which we are  incapable of  the mantis shrimp can see a greater  spectrum of rays and waves and possibly  some great unknown but perhaps it is  better that its abilities remain a  mystery  at this time we are probably not ready  for such visual capabilities  our current ones have already proven to  be overbearing  maybe the best things in life are not  meant to be seen because they must be  felt or understood there is a seven  percent acceptance rate at the  university of chicago and whilst these  essays make up a part of the application  they'd also be looking at grades and  other materials and they even have an  optional sat  the university of chicago is not the  only college with weird prompts here are  some from other places we have if you  could choose to be raised by robots  dinosaurs or aliens who would you pick  and why  you have just finished your 300 page  autobiography  please submit page 217.  for this one you have 150 words  take a risk perhaps you could speak from  experience or imagination in answering  you've just reached your one millionth  hit on your youtube video  what is the video about  and this one from yale what is something  about which you have changed your mind  in the last three years upon reflection  if you don't have an answer for that one  then perhaps you weren't as open-minded  as you thought you were  these essays are one way that you can  show that you are well-rounded and  interested in a variety of topics and if  you are someone who wants to foster  those interests and hobbies then check  out skillshare who have kindly sponsored  this video skillshare is an online  learning community with thousands of  online classes  it's a place to get inspired learn new  skills and put them to work in impactful  ways i was interested in joining  skillshare to learn ways to make my  content and workflow better i enjoyed  thomas frank's course mastering  productivity create a custom system that  works  it made me feel like i was about to be  entering my organized era there's a  special link down in the description and  the first 1000 people who click it will  get a one month free trial of skillshare  thanks for watching this video and a  special shout out to today's patreon cat  of the day rio jane  [Music]  you
 

